    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe structure is clean, additive, and idiomatic for this codebase:\n\n- **New `FiscalYear` class placed correctly.** It sits in `rest/function/common/` alongside the other pure, stateless helpers (`Luhn`, `Localities`, `Strings`, `Hashes`), which is exactly where a shared date-arithmetic utility belongs. It is not bolted onto an existing class \u2014 and correctly kept separate from the existing date helper `AgeBand`, since fiscal-year basis and age banding are distinct concerns.\n- **Cohesive, single-purpose unit.** `of` / `yy` / `label` / `between` are a tight, non-overlapping API layered on one definition (`of`). The class docstring makes it the single source of truth so the three derivations (membership number segment, `fiscalYear` field, tenure) can't drift \u2014 which is exactly the right place for that logic to live rather than being duplicated across three MapStruct expressions.\n- **Mapper delegates rather than inlines.** The `@Mapping` expressions call `FiscalYear.yy` / `FiscalYear.label` using fully-qualified names, matching the established pattern in this file (`Luhn.checkDigit`, `IdentityKey.ofOwner`, `CustomerCode.regionOf`). The `membershipPoints` default method uses the imported `FiscalYear.between`, replacing the inline `ChronoUnit.DAYS` computation cleanly, and the now-unused `ChronoUnit` import was removed.\n- **Docs kept in sync additively.** The OpenAPI schema and the `membershipPoints` Javadoc were updated in step with the behaviour change, with no leftover references to the old 365-day basis.\n\nNo duplication, no catch-all growth, logic is in the right unit. A maintainer of this codebase would find this natural.",
